.he "chown (1)                    9/30/26                    chown (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
chown
- change file ownership
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
chown uid file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Chown changes the owner of each named file to uid, which may be a
decimal user I.D. or a user name looked up in /etc/passwd.  The group
of each file is carried through unchanged.
.sp
Only the super-user may change the owner of a file.
.sp
This is the v7 interface, without the dash micronix put on it.
Owner (1) is the same command under micronix's own name, and it
reports an owner as well as setting one:
.bd
chown root a.out
and
.bd
owner -root a.out
do the same thing.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A user name is looked up with getpwnam, which is in libc - see
getpwent (3).
.sp
Chown here sets an owner and a group at once, the pair packed as
uid | gid << 8, and has no -1 to leave a field alone.  So each file
is stat'd first and its group carried through.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/passwd - user I.D. and name relationships
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
owner (1), chgrp (1), group (1), chmod (1), ls (1)
